Yes, it's very typical — in fact, it's the **idiomatic convention** recommended by [The Rust Programming Language book](https://doc.rust-lang.org/book/ch11-03-test-organization.html).

**Unit tests** go in the same file as the implementation, inside a `#[cfg(test)]` block:

```rust
#[cfg(test)]
mod tests {
    use super::*;

    #[test]
    fn some_test() {
        // ...
    }
}
```

The `#[cfg(test)]` attribute ensures the test module is **compiled only during `cargo test`**, so it has zero overhead in release builds. The `use super::*;` gives access to private items from the parent module, which is the whole point — you can test internal details without making them `pub`.

**Integration tests** (black-box, public-API-only tests) go in a separate `tests/` directory at the crate root, where each file is compiled as its own crate.

So the pattern you're seeing in the harness-protocol crate (tests in `commands.rs`, `effects.rs`, `events.rs`) follows standard Rust practice. Everything in the same file keeps unit tests close to the code they exercise, making them easy to find and maintain.

